WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.801
-I'm so happy
you're back on the show.

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.400
Thank you so much
for being our guest.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.434
And I wanted to thank you
because I was lucky enough

00:00:06.434 --> 00:00:10.367
to be on "CBS Sunday Morning,"
where you interviewed me.

00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:12.767
-And that was really fun.
-It was fun.

00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:14.667
You were in my home.

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:18.267
-I was in your home
and I was in your lair,

00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:21.033
your sanctum sanctorum,
your vinyl room.

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:24.100
-Yes, my record --
-Which is amazing.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.968
And not just because you have
an amazing library of LPs,

00:00:27.968 --> 00:00:30.100
but really because
you have a whole section

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.634
devoted to show tunes,
to Broadway shows.

00:00:32.634 --> 00:00:35.400
So objectivity
was out the window then.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.033
-Really? Yeah.

00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:38.834
Did you expect -- Do you
learn more about people

00:00:38.834 --> 00:00:40.000
when you interview
them in their homes?

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.400
-Yeah, I think so.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.367
I mean, listen, when I've been
on this show before --

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:46.868
and I really mean this -- your
love of music is so palpable.

00:00:46.868 --> 00:00:48.801
I can feel it when bands are on,
you know,

00:00:48.801 --> 00:00:50.067
and the camera's not on you.

00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:51.901
I can feel how into it you are,

00:00:51.901 --> 00:00:54.367
but being in the vinyl room,
I could really feel it

00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:55.968
and your love of it.

00:00:55.968 --> 00:00:58.067
and just, like,
the equipment and everything

00:00:58.067 --> 00:00:59.300
and the dusting
of the records.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.000
I mean, you really
care about it.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.934
And after the show,
a bus will be leaving

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:05.434
to go out to your vinyl room,
so everyone --

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:08.467
-Yeah, everyone come to my house
and to my little room.

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:09.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:09.901 --> 00:01:12.734
Here's a photo of us
in the vinyl room.

00:01:12.734 --> 00:01:16.400
I think we were dancing.
-To "Little Shop of Horrors."

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.367
-"Little Shop of Horrors."
-As one does.

00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:19.400
-That's right.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:20.567
You found
"Little Shop of Horrors."

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:22.334
You go, "Let's put this one on,"
because I'm like,

00:01:22.334 --> 00:01:23.601
"Listen to this system,
it's so good."

00:01:23.601 --> 00:01:25.367
And we were playing like
Led Zeppelin or whatever.

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:26.634
And I go, "Isn't that crazy?"

00:01:26.634 --> 00:01:28.400
And you go,
"We got to listen to this."

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.467
And then you put that on,
and we both started dancing,

00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:31.501
'cause, like...

00:01:31.501 --> 00:01:32.634
♪ Little shop ♪

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:34.000
♪ Little shop of horrors ♪

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.133
♪ Little shop ♪

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:38.534
Oh, that was so good.
Oh, it was so fun.

00:01:38.534 --> 00:01:41.200
I want to talk
about "Mobituaries."

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.667
It's a fascinating show.

00:01:42.667 --> 00:01:46.300
I know it sounds a little
morbid, being about obituaries,

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.100
but it's not actually.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.634
It's very interesting and it's
charming, actually, and nice.

00:01:51.634 --> 00:01:53.334
-Listen,
I'm a fan of obituaries,

00:01:53.334 --> 00:01:54.367
which I know sounds weird.

00:01:54.367 --> 00:01:55.601
I got it from my father,

00:01:55.601 --> 00:01:58.334
and my father was not
a gloomy person at all.

00:01:58.334 --> 00:02:00.534
He was a very, like,
buoyant, optimistic person.

00:02:00.534 --> 00:02:01.667
-Why did he like it?

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:04.634
-Because a good obituary
is about someone's life.

00:02:04.634 --> 00:02:06.100
It's not about their death.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.000
And a good one, when I read it,
it's kind of like a trailer

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.634
for an Oscar-winning biopic.

00:02:10.634 --> 00:02:12.467
It kind of has
a sweep and a romance,

00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:14.868
like, the highs, the lows,
the triumphs, you know,

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:17.267
the hopefully
not too many tragedies.

00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:19.567
But, yeah,
that's how a good one reads.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.901
I mean, I'm not that interested
in how people die.

00:02:21.901 --> 00:02:23.834
I'm interested kind of
in their beginnings

00:02:23.834 --> 00:02:25.868
and sort of
the arc of their lives.

00:02:25.868 --> 00:02:29.000
But it's a great beat,
and, I mean, on newspapers,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.467
the ones that still
have obit departments,

00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:32.667
it's really kind of coveted,

00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:34.267
because
if you're writing for it,

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:35.934
you're writing for,
like, the sports section,

00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:39.434
for entertainment, for business,
depending on who dies.

00:02:39.434 --> 00:02:40.601
-Wow. Yeah, you're right.
-Yeah.

00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:42.267
-And it's always a downer
at the end there, but --

00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:44.767
-No. Well, sometimes,
it's not even about people.

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:48.968
Sometimes, it's about the death
of things or an idea.

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:50.968
Like, last week was --

00:02:50.968 --> 00:02:54.334
-The last week's episode
was the death of

00:02:54.334 --> 00:02:56.701
the old-timey Hollywood accent.

00:02:56.701 --> 00:03:00.534
So, I mean, anyone certainly
under a certain age,

00:03:00.534 --> 00:03:02.334
if you turn on, say,
Turner Classic Movies,

00:03:02.334 --> 00:03:05.133
black-and-white movies,
everyone's speaking in something

00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:08.167
that has commonly been called
the Mid-Atlantic accent.

00:03:08.167 --> 00:03:09.567
Like, they don't pronounce
their R's.

00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:11.334
They say things like,
"It's mah-velous."

00:03:11.334 --> 00:03:13.367
And you're like,
"What is that accent?"

00:03:13.367 --> 00:03:16.100
And, you know, like,
Bette Davis, Orson Welles,

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.567
all these great stars --
Katharine Hepburn had it.

00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:20.334
And then it just
sort of disappeared.

00:03:20.334 --> 00:03:21.634
And it's interesting.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:24.133
It was a real accent, though.
It was not a put-on.

00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:27.300
People back then, in the
Northeast of the country

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.234
especially,
really talked that way.

00:03:29.234 --> 00:03:30.434
-Yeah. And why, though?

00:03:30.434 --> 00:03:34.067
Because it was --
You traced it back to theater?

00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:35.200
-Well, some of it, yeah.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.334
So, what happened is, over time,

00:03:37.334 --> 00:03:40.701
the R's kind of were sanded off
the ends of people's words.

00:03:40.701 --> 00:03:43.200
They just really started talking
in that way where they --

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.167
-They go "thah."
-Yeah, that. Yeah, exactly.

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:46.634
Yeah, "thah."
-Look out "thah."

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:48.100
-Yeah. What a wonderful
"yea" this is.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.334
-Yeah, what a "yea."
We're not "heah." We're "thah."

00:03:50.334 --> 00:03:52.701
-Or, you know -- Or the donkey
in "Winnie the Pooh"

00:03:52.701 --> 00:03:54.000
was "Eeyoh."

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.667
It wasn't like "Eeyore."
You didn't pronounce your R's.

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:57.968
And it just kind of went away.

00:03:57.968 --> 00:04:00.100
And in the theater,
it was really effective

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.200
because, you know,
the accent also involved

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.200
enunciating,
kind of elongated vowels.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.100
And it would travel
to the back of the house

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.701
when there were no microphones.

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:11.267
And then Hollywood adopted it.

00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:13.334
But there were people,
like the Roosevelts,

00:04:13.334 --> 00:04:16.467
you know,
who really spoke in this way.

00:04:16.467 --> 00:04:20.267
And then Marlon Brando just kind
of drove a stake through it --

00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:23.968
-Yeah. "I'm not doing this."
-Yeah, he wasn't going to do it.

00:04:23.968 --> 00:04:26.000
So, he was in
"Streetcar Named Desire"

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.601
in 1951, in the movie.

00:04:27.601 --> 00:04:29.901
And he didn't say like,
"Stellah, Stellah!"

00:04:29.901 --> 00:04:30.934
Like, he talked like
a normal person.

00:04:30.934 --> 00:04:32.100
-Can you imagine?

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.501
Yeah, it was like,
"Stellah, Stellah.

00:04:34.501 --> 00:04:36.767
What are you doing up thah?

00:04:36.767 --> 00:04:39.067
Stella!"
Yeah, exactly.

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:41.367
Guys, there's more stories
like that and more.

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:44.701
You're going to love it.
Mo Rocca, "Mobituaries."

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:47.901
Listen to it wherever
you get your podcasts --

00:04:47.901 --> 00:04:49.968
iHeart, whatever you want to do.

00:04:49.968 --> 00:04:51.601
We'll be right back with
a performance from Bleachers.

00:04:51.601 --> 00:04:52.801
Stick around.

